{"parentUuid":null,"isSidechain":true,"promptId":"e6c71aa2-85de-41b4-a940-039142d474f6","agentId":"a69dc108738e5f780","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":"Build a calendar (.ics) of weekly 2-hour project work blocks for Eric Rash (Baylor Athletics, America/Chicago), placed into open slots of his real work calendar. Do NOT git commit, do NOT edit portfolio.json, do NOT create or modify any calendar events (read-only calendar access). Today is 2026-09-14 (Monday evening).\n\n## Inputs\n- Slate/charters: `~/DEV/project-triage/portfolio.json` (read only). Projects in `projects[]` with `charter` (receiver, done_means, steps[{name,due,done}]).\n- Work calendar: Google Calendar MCP tool `mcp__claude_ai_Google_Calendar__list_events` with calendarId `3gso454ipti1qgbb09532u55km3ncm4g@import.calendar.google.com` (his Outlook work calendar imported). Load it via ToolSearch (\"select:mcp__claude_ai_Google_Calendar__list_events\") if needed. Fetch 2026-09-15 → 2026-12-11 in chunks of ~2 weeks (timeZone America/Chicago, orderBy startTime, pageSize 250, follow nextPageToken). Keep only start, end, summary. Events with no summary are private busy time (e.g., 12:00–13:00 daily, 17:00–17:30) — still busy. Save the trimmed snapshot to `/Users/kristinarash/.claude/jobs/df3b3576/tmp/work-calendar-busy.json`.\n- The calendar has recurring meetings (Cheer training Tue/Thu 06:00–08:30, Athletics Research Meeting Thu 08:45–09:30, Staff Meeting Thu 09:30–10:30, Care Team Tue 13:00–13:45, Student-Athlete Services Leadership Check-in Mon 11:30–12:15) and one-off \"Focus time\" events (auto-booked ~2 weeks ahead, times vary, e.g. Mon 8–10, Tue 8:30–10:30, Wed 7–9, Thu 13–15, Fri 7–9). Future weeks will mostly lack Focus time events.\n\n## Assignment (decided by Eric)\nOne 2-hour block per project per week, weekday preference:\n- Mon: Tech_Inventory_Analysis through the week of 2026-10-05 (finishes 10-09); from 2026-10-12 Mon goes to NCAA_BestPractices_Doc (next up).\n- Tue: HPI_Protocol · Wed: Education_Curriculum · Thu: RTP_Constraints_Course · Fri: WomensHealth_Survey.\n- Date range: Tue 2026-09-15 through Fri 2026-12-11 (this week's Mon 9/14 is past — put that week's Tech Inventory block on its best open slot Tue–Fri of the same week, not double-booking). Skip Thanksgiving 2026-11-26 and 11-27 (move those blocks earlier in that week if an open slot exists, else skip and report).\n\n## Placement rule (Eric: \"move to fit the open slots\")\nFor each (week, project): on the preferred weekday,\n1. If a \"Focus time\" event exists that day and no other project block is already in it: place the block at the Focus time start (2h; if the focus event is shorter than 2h, still start there only if the full 2h is free).\n2. Else the first fully free 2-hour window scanning 08:00, 08:30, … 14:30, then 07:30 — bounded 07:30–16:30, never overlapping any busy event (all events count as busy EXCEPT \"Focus time\", which is free time) or another placed block.\n3. If none that day, try the other weekdays of that week in order Mon→Fri (only days on/after today for this week), then report it if still unplaced.\n\n## Deliverables\n1. `~/DEV/project-triage/slate_calendar.py`: pure functions (e.g. `plan_blocks(events, assignment, start, end, holidays)` and `to_ics(blocks, charters)`), plus a CLI: `python3 slate_calendar.py --events <busy.json> --out <file.ics> [--registry portfolio.json]`. Standard library only (zoneinfo is fine), Python 3.9 compatible (use `from __future__ import annotations`). Match the repo's style (look at slate.py, portfolio.py).\n2. `~/DEV/project-triage/tests/test_slate_calendar.py` (unittest, like tests/test_slate.py) covering: prefers Focus time; avoids busy events; skips a slot overlapping lunch; falls to another weekday when the day is full; holiday skip; correct UTC conversion on both sides of the 2026-11-01 DST change; ICS has CRLF line endings, BEGIN/END VCALENDAR, one VEVENT per block, stable UIDs, lines folded at 75 octets. Run `python3 -m unittest discover -s tests -t .` from the repo — the whole suite must stay green.\n3. The ICS: `SUMMARY: Project block — <friendly name>` (Tech Inventory / HPI protocol v1 / Education curriculum / Return to Performance course / Women's health survey / NCAA best practices). DESCRIPTION: receiver, done means, next undone step + due date from the charter at generation time, and the line \"Debrief it in /interview-me.\" DTSTART/DTEND in UTC (Z). UID `slate-<Project>-<YYYYMMDD>@erash11`. CATEGORIES:Project block. TRANSP:OPAQUE. PRODID/VERSION/CALSCALE/METHOD:PUBLISH headers.\n   Write it to `~/Library/CloudStorage/GoogleDrive-eric.rash11@gmail.com/My Drive/00_Primary_Storage_Drive/05_SYSTEMS/ProjectBlocks/fall-2026-project-blocks.ics` (create ProjectBlocks/ if 05_SYSTEMS exists; if 05_SYSTEMS does not exist, use `00_Primary_Storage_Drive/ProjectBlocks/`). Verify it parses: count VEVENTs, and re-read the first 2 events.\n\n## Report back (≤400 words)\nExact ICS path; total blocks; test result line; a table of the first 2 weeks (date, time, project); every block that moved off its preferred weekday or was skipped, with the reason; any assumption you made. Note in one line that future weeks were planned against recurring meetings only (Focus time not booked yet), so regenerating in ~2 weeks will re-fit.\n<context_window_protection>\n  <priority_instructions>\n    Every byte a tool returns enters your conversation memory and costs reasoning capacity for the rest of the session. The context-mode tools let you do the work in a sandbox and surface only the derived answer — the raw bytes stay out. Think-in-Code: program the analysis, do not compute it by reading raw data into your conversation.\n  </priority_instructions>\n\n  <deferred_tool_bootstrap>\n    The context-mode tools below may be DEFERRED in your harness — their schemas are not loaded yet, so calling them directly fails (e.g. \"tool not found\" / InputValidationError). Load them ONCE before your first ctx_* call:\n    ToolSearch(query: \"select:mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_batch_execute,mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_search,mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute,mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute_file,mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_fetch_and_index\")\n    After that they are callable. If any ctx_* call fails as not-found, ToolSearch it and retry — do NOT fall back to Bash/Read just because the schema was not loaded yet.\n  </deferred_tool_bootstrap>\n\n  <tool_selection_hierarchy>\n    0. MEMORY: mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_search(sort: \"timeline\")\n       - On resume or compaction, query prior decisions, errors, plans, user prompts before asking the user — auto-captured session memory is searchable.\n    1. GATHER: mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_batch_execute(commands, queries)\n       - Primary research tool. Runs commands in parallel, auto-indexes each output, and (when queries are passed) returns matching sections in the same round trip — no follow-up search call.\n       - Each command: {label: \"section header\", command: \"shell command\"}; the label becomes the FTS5 chunk title — descriptive labels improve search.\n    2. FOLLOW-UP: mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_search(queries: [\"q1\", \"q2\", ...])\n       - Multiple related questions about anything already indexed (your captures + session memory). Batch every question in one array; the ranking pipeline runs per-query and the round-trip cost is paid once.\n    3. PROCESSING: mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute(language, code) | mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute_file(path, language, code)\n       - Derive answers FROM data: filter, count, aggregate, parse, transform. Only what you console.log() enters your conversation; the raw bytes stay in the sandbox.\n  </tool_selection_hierarchy>\n\n  <when_not_to_use>\n    - You intend to PROCESS the output (filter, count, parse, aggregate) → use mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_batch_execute or mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute. Bash stays correct when you intend to OBSERVE a short fixed output (git status on a clean tree, whoami, pwd) or when you are mutating state (git, mkdir, rm, mv, navigation).\n    - You want to analyze, summarize, or extract from a file → use mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute_file. Read stays correct when you intend to Edit the file (Edit needs the exact bytes in your conversation to match against).\n    - WebFetch → use mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_fetch_and_index; full network access, results indexed for mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_search, raw page bytes never enter your conversation.\n    - mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute and mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute_file for file writes → these run code in a subprocess and discard the sandbox FS; they are for analysis, processing, and computation only.\n  </when_not_to_use>\n\n  <file_writing_policy>\n    File writes use the native Write or Edit tool — mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute, mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute_file, and Bash subprocesses do not persist edits to the host filesystem.\n    Applies to all file types: code, configs, plans, specs, YAML, JSON, markdown.\n  </file_writing_policy>\n\n  <output_constraints>\n    <artifact_policy>\n      Write artifacts (code, configs, PRDs) to files. Return only: file path + 1-line description.\n    </artifact_policy>\n  </output_constraints>\n  <session_continuity>\n    Skills, roles, and decisions captured earlier in this session are a memory aid, not a standing order. Treat them as context that may help — the user's most recent message always takes precedence. If a captured directive conflicts with what the user now asks, follow the user; a past phrase does not bind you.\n  </session_continuity>\n\n</context_window_protection>"},"uuid":"0b38cfe4-7371-4eb9-b531-1427af954882","timestamp":"2026-09-15T02:32:46.709Z","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV","sessionId":"df3b3576-b317-408d-964a-187d57ed18ca","version":"2.1.271","gitBranch":"main"}
